        prose: [
          "Statute 741.2901(1) means the office on the other side of a domestic case is a specialized one. Each state attorney is required to develop special units or assign prosecutors to specialize in the prosecution of domestic violence cases, with the qualification written into the same sentence that such specialization need not be an exclusive area of duty assignment, and those prosecutors and their support staff are required to receive training in domestic violence issues. The office holding that assignment here is the Office of the State Attorney for the Thirteenth Judicial Circuit, and Statute 741.2901(2) is the provision directing the state attorney in each circuit to adopt a pro-prosecution policy for acts of domestic violence as defined in Statute 741.28.",
          "Which clerk division holds the file follows the grading of the underlying offense rather than the domestic designation. A battery graded a misdemeanor of the first degree under Statute 784.03(1)(b) and domestic battery by strangulation, graded a felony of the third degree by Statute 784.041(3), sit in different divisions, the misdemeanor in county criminal and the felony in circuit criminal. The Hillsborough County Clerk lists county criminal alongside circuit criminal at its courthouse locations, with circuit criminal listed at the Edgecomb Courthouse, 800 E. Twiggs Street, Room 101, Tampa, and criminal courts at the Plant City Courthouse, 301 North Michigan Avenue, Room 1071.",
          "Statute 741.2901(3) puts work in front of the first appearance that is separate from the charging document. The State Attorney's Office is directed to investigate the defendant's history and to present it at first appearance, when setting bond, and when passing sentence. A version of that history is in the room before a defense lawyer has said anything about it. Reaching counsel ahead of that hearing is what puts a second reading of the same history in the room, and it does not depend on the court calendar."
        ]
