        statute: {
          citation: "Florida Statute 800.04, the 2025 Florida Statutes",
          body: "The section is titled Lewd or lascivious offenses committed upon or in the presence of persons less than 16 years of age, and it is built in layers. Subsection (1) is definitions, and it carries one that people do not expect: the definition of sexual activity excludes an act done for a bona fide medical purpose. Subsections (2) and (3) then remove five arguments by name, before any limb is reached. Subsection (2), headed PROHIBITED DEFENSES, provides that neither the victim's lack of chastity nor the victim's consent is a defense to the crimes proscribed by the section. Subsection (3), headed IGNORANCE OR BELIEF OF VICTIM'S AGE, provides that the perpetrator's ignorance of the victim's age, the victim's misrepresentation of his or her age, or the perpetrator's bona fide belief of the victim's age cannot be raised as a defense in a prosecution under the section. Those two subsections are the reason the sentence people most want to say is the sentence the statute has already addressed, and it is better to know that at the beginning than to learn it at a hearing. Subsection (4), LEWD OR LASCIVIOUS BATTERY, provides that a person commits lewd or lascivious battery by engaging in sexual activity with a person 12 years of age or older but less than 16 years of age, or by encouraging, forcing, or enticing any person less than 16 years of age to engage in sadomasochistic abuse, sexual bestiality, prostitution, or any other act involving sexual activity. Read the second branch carefully: it has no lower age bound and it requires no sexual activity by the person charged. Subsection (5), LEWD OR LASCIVIOUS MOLESTATION, provides that a person who intentionally touches in a lewd or lascivious manner the breasts, genitals, genital area, or buttocks, or the clothing covering them, of a person less than 16 years of age, or forces or entices a person under 16 years of age to so touch the perpetrator, commits lewd or lascivious molestation. Subsection (6), LEWD OR LASCIVIOUS CONDUCT, reaches a person who intentionally touches a person under 16 years of age in a lewd or lascivious manner, or who solicits a person under 16 years of age to commit a lewd or lascivious act. Subsection (7), LEWD OR LASCIVIOUS EXHIBITION, reaches a person who intentionally masturbates, who intentionally exposes the genitals in a lewd or lascivious manner, or who intentionally commits any other sexual act that does not involve actual physical or sexual contact with the victim, including, but not limited to, sadomasochistic abuse, sexual bestiality, or the simulation of any act involving sexual activity, in the presence of a victim who is less than 16 years of age. The words about presence are closing language, so they attach to each of the three branches rather than to the last one. Subsection (8), headed EXCEPTION, provides that a mother's breastfeeding of her baby does not under any circumstance constitute a violation of the section. The structural fact that runs through the whole of it is that two ages are doing the work at the same time, the age of the person alleged to be the victim and the age of the person charged, and they are graded in different paragraphs rather than in one place. Subsection (5) alone runs from a life felony where the offender is 18 or older and the victim is less than 12, to a third degree felony where the offender is less than 18 and the victim is 12 to 15, for conduct the statute describes in a single sentence at paragraph (5)(a). The section's History note ends at s. 7, ch. 2022-165."
        }
