            note: "Statute 782.04(1)(a), first degree murder, on each of the routes at its subparagraphs 1., 2. and 3.: premeditated design under subparagraph 1., a killing committed by a person engaged in the perpetration of or attempt to perpetrate an enumerated felony under subparagraph 2., or a death resulting from the unlawful distribution by a person 18 years of age or older of a listed substance under subparagraph 3. The stated reason for the empty level cell is one sentence of Statute 921.002: The Criminal Punishment Code shall apply to all felony offenses, except capital felonies, committed on or after October 1, 1998. The chart at Statute 921.0022 carries no row for completed first degree murder; its 782.04(1) entry is at level 9 and reads Attempt, conspire, or solicit to commit premeditated murder, which is the inchoate offense. The term comes from Statute 775.082(1)(a), which opens except as provided in paragraph (b) and provides that a person who has been convicted of a capital felony shall be punished by death if the proceeding held to determine sentence according to the procedure set forth in s. 921.141 results in a determination that such person shall be punished by death, otherwise such person shall be punished by life imprisonment and shall be ineligible for parole. Paragraph (b) is the separate track for a capital felony under s. 782.04 committed before the person attained 18 years of age, and it is read before paragraph (a) is applied to a young defendant. Subparagraph (1)(b)1. covers a person who actually killed, intended to kill, or attempted to kill the victim: a term of imprisonment for life if, after a sentencing hearing conducted by the court in accordance with s. 921.1401, the court finds that life imprisonment is an appropriate sentence, and a term of imprisonment of at least 40 years if the court finds that life imprisonment is not an appropriate sentence. Subparagraph (1)(b)2. covers a person who did not actually kill, intend to kill, or attempt to kill the victim, and provides that such a person may be punished by a term of imprisonment for life or by a term of years equal to life on the same finding. Subparagraph (1)(b)3. does not stop there: it requires the court to make a written finding as to whether the person is eligible for a sentence review hearing under s. 921.1402(2)(a) or (c), based on that same question of whether the person actually killed, intended to kill, or attempted to kill the victim."
